  "cfg": {
    "locomotion": {
      "mode": "static",
      "allowedRadius": 0,
      "turnMode": "snap"
    },
    "outline": {
      "version": 1,
      "course": {
        "title": "Viruses: Structure, Classification & What It Means to Be Alive",
        "goal": "Students can describe what a virus is, explain why viruses depend on a host cell, identify the parts of a virus, and classify any virus using capsid shape, envelope, and nucleic acid type.",
        "paceNote": "Learning outline generated from the knowledge graph"
      },
      "progress": {
        "completedSectionIds": []
      },
      "chapters": [
        {
          "id": "ch_msgqjc7dghi0",
          "title": "What Is a Virus?",
          "summary": "Establish the virion as a tiny non-cell that depends on a host, and confront why classifying viruses as alive is controversial.",
          "sections": [
            {
              "id": "sec_msgqjc7ddwvi",
              "title": "Meet the Virion",
              "type": "reading",
              "summary": "Characteristics of Viruses passage (virion definition, lack of cell parts, example diseases).",
              "purpose": "Introduce the virion, its tiny size, and the fact that it is not a cell and causes disease.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": [
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqju3wdoay",
                    "title": "What Is a Virion?",
                    "html": "<p>A single virus particle is called a <strong>virion</strong>. It is astonishingly tiny — much smaller than a prokaryotic cell (like a bacterium), which is already one of the smallest living things.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "How does the size of a virion compare to a prokaryotic cell?",
                      "options": [
                        "Larger than a prokaryotic cell",
                        "Smaller than a prokaryotic cell",
                        "The same size",
                        "Larger than a eukaryotic cell"
                      ],
                      "answer": "Smaller than a prokaryotic cell",
                      "explanation": "A virion is a tiny particle much smaller than a prokaryotic cell."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "Scale comparison diagram: a large eukaryotic cell, a smaller bacterium, and a much smaller virion labeled, showing relative sizes"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqju3whyt6",
                    "title": "A Virus Is NOT a Cell",
                    "html": "<p>Here's the key surprise: a virus is <strong>not made of cells at all</strong>. Because it isn't a cell, a virus has <em>no cell membrane, no cytoplasm, and no organelles</em> like ribosomes.</p><p>So a virion is basically just genetic material wrapped in a protein coat — not a living cell.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "A student says a virus is 'just a really tiny living cell.' Using what you just read, explain what's wrong with that idea.",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "A virus is not a cell at all — it has no cell membrane, no cytoplasm, and no organelles like ribosomes. It's only genetic material inside a protein coat.",
                      "explanation": "Viruses lack the very structures that define a cell, so calling one a tiny cell is incorrect."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "A 'cell parts checklist' next to a virion: checkboxes for membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes all marked X, only 'genetic material + protein coat' checked"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqju3wjwj3",
                    "title": "Why This Matters: No Cell = No Solo Reproduction",
                    "html": "<p>Because a virus lacks ribosomes and other cell parts, it <strong>cannot make its own proteins or copy itself</strong>. On its own, a lone virion just sits there — it cannot grow or multiply.</p><p>To reproduce, a virus must get <em>inside a host cell</em> and use that cell's machinery. This is the whole reason a virus depends on a host.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "Imagine a virion sealed alone in a sterile jar with plenty of water and sugar but no living cells. Will it multiply? Why or why not?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "No. A virus can't reproduce by itself because it has no ribosomes or cell machinery. It needs a host cell to make copies, and there are no host cells in the jar.",
                      "explanation": "This re-skins the idea: without a host cell's machinery, a lone virion cannot make proteins or reproduce."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "Two panels: (1) lone virion outside a cell with a red X over a 'reproduce' arrow; (2) same virion inside a host cell with a green check, new virions being made"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqju3w2csi",
                    "title": "Viruses Cause Disease",
                    "html": "<p>Viruses infect and live inside the cells of living organisms. When they invade a host's cells, they can cause disease.</p><p>Familiar examples include <strong>AIDS, influenza (flu), chicken pox, and the common cold</strong>.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "Which of these is a disease caused by a virus?",
                      "options": [
                        "A broken bone",
                        "The common cold",
                        "Sunburn",
                        "Scurvy"
                      ],
                      "answer": "The common cold",
                      "explanation": "The common cold, along with AIDS, flu, and chicken pox, is caused by a virus."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqju3wp9ma",
                    "title": "The Big Idea to Carry Forward",
                    "html": "<p>Put it all together: <strong>a virus is not a cell — it's just genetic material inside a protein coat.</strong> That single fact explains why it can't reproduce alone and must hijack a host cell, and why it can make us sick.</p><p>Remember this structure and you can reason about <em>any</em> virus, not just memorize each one.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "In one or two sentences, explain why every virus needs a host cell to reproduce — based on what a virus actually is.",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "A virus is not a cell; it's only genetic material inside a protein coat, so it has no machinery to make proteins or copy itself. It must use a host cell's machinery to reproduce.",
                      "explanation": "This states the aha insight: the non-cellular structure of a virus is the root cause of its dependence on a host."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  }
                ]
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "",
                "html": "",
                "status": "idle",
                "followUp": null
              },
              "quiz": {
                "items": []
              },
              "covers": [
                "n1",
                "n2",
                "n4"
              ],
              "role": "opening",
              "sourceHint": "Characteristics of Viruses passage (virion definition, lack of cell parts, example diseases).",
              "figureIds": [],
              "installsAha": [
                "aha1"
              ],
              "buildStatus": "done"
            },
            {
              "id": "sec_msgqjc7dq03z",
              "title": "Cell or Not? The Reproduction Test",
              "type": "h5",
              "summary": "Characteristics of Viruses — 'they must depend on a host cell to synthesize their proteins and to make copies of themselves.'",
              "purpose": "Let students discover that a virion has no cell parts and cannot reproduce without a host by building and testing both a bacterium and a virion.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": []
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "Build a 2D 'Build & Test' lab widget with two side-by-side assembly zones: a BACTERIUM cell outline (left) and a VIRION outline (right). Above them sits a PARTS TRAY with draggable tokens: [Cell Membrane], [Cytoplasm], [Ribosomes], [DNA/Genetic Material], [Protein Coat (Capsid)]. STEP 1 PREDICT: before building, ask the student a checkbox prediction — 'Which object do you think can reproduce on its own?' with options Bacterium / Virion / Both. Record the answer silently. STEP 2 BUILD: the student drags parts into each zone. The bacterium zone accepts ALL five parts and glows green when membrane+cytoplasm+ribosomes+DNA are placed. The virion zone REJECTS membrane, cytoplasm, and ribosomes (token bounces back with a small note 'A virus has no cell parts') and only accepts Protein Coat + Genetic Material — showing the virion is just genetic material inside a coat. STEP 3 TEST — press 'Reproduce'. The completed bacterium animates splitting into two (success ✓). The lone virion pulses, shows 'No ribosomes → cannot make proteins → cannot copy itself', and FAILS (✗). A new HOST CELL appears; the student drags the virion INTO the host cell, presses 'Reproduce' again, and now the virion succeeds — multiple copies burst out of the host, labeled 'Used the host's ribosomes'. STEP 4 REVEAL: compare the student's Step-1 prediction to the result. If they predicted the virion could reproduce alone, show 'Surprised? A virion is NOT a tiny living cell — it can only copy itself by hijacking a host.' Include a running summary line: 'Virion = genetic material + protein coat. No cell parts → no self-reproduction.' Provide a Reset button. Use simple flat 2D biology shapes, clear labels, and accessible drag targets.",
                "html": "<div id=\"lab\" style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;max-width:900px;margin:auto;padding:12px;background:#f4f8fb;color:#123\">\n<h2 style=\"text-align:center;color:#0a4\">Cell or Not? The Reproduction Test</h2>\n\n<div id=\"step1\" style=\"background:#fff;border:2px solid #0a4;border-radius:8px;padding:10px;margin-bottom:10px\">\n<b>STEP 1 — Predict:</b> Which object can reproduce on its own?\n<label style=\"margin:0 8px\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"pred\" value=\"Bacterium\">Bacterium</label>\n<label style=\"margin:0 8px\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"pred\" value=\"Virion\">Virion</label>\n<label style=\"margin:0 8px\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"pred\" value=\"Both\">Both</label>\n<button onclick=\"lockPred()\" style=\"padding:4px 10px\">Lock in</button>\n<span id=\"predMsg\" style=\"color:#080;font-weight:bold\"></span>\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"tray\" style=\"background:#fff;border:1px dashed #888;border-radius:8px;padding:8px;margin-bottom:10px\">\n<b>Parts Tray</b> (drag into a zone):<br>\n<div id=\"trayTokens\" style=\"margin-top:6px\"></div>\n</div>\n\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap\">\n<div id=\"bactZone\" class=\"zone\" style=\"flex:1;min-width:260px;min-height:180px;border:3px solid #0a4;border-radius:40px;background:#eafff0;padding:8px;position:relative\">\n<b>BACTERIUM</b><div class=\"drop\"></div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"virZone\" class=\"zone\" style=\"flex:1;min-width:260px;min-height:180px;border:3px solid #a05;border-radius:20px;background:#fff0f6;padding:8px;position:relative\">\n<b>VIRION</b><div class=\"drop\"></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"hostWrap\" style=\"display:none;margin-top:10px\">\n<div id=\"hostZone\" class=\"zone\" style=\"min-height:120px;border:3px dashed #06a;border-radius:60px;background:#e8f2ff;padding:8px\">\n<b>HOST CELL</b> (drag the virion inside)<div class=\"drop\"></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<div style=\"text-align:center;margin:12px\">\n<button id=\"repBtn\" onclick=\"reproduce()\" style=\"padding:8px 18px;font-size:16px;background:#0a4;color:#fff;border:none;border-radius:6px\">Reproduce ▶</button>\n<button onclick=\"location.reload()\" style=\"padding:8px 14px;margin-left:8px\">Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"result\" style=\"min-height:24px;text-align:center;font-weight:bold\"></div>\n<div id=\"reveal\" style=\"text-align:center;color:#a05;font-weight:bold;margin-top:6px\"></div>\n<div style=\"text-align:center;margin-top:10px;padding:8px;background:#123;color:#fff;border-radius:6px\">\nVirion = genetic material + protein coat. No cell parts → no self-reproduction.\n</div>\n\n<style>\n.tok{display:inline-block;margin:4px;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:6px;background:#ffd;border:1px solid #aa0;cursor:grab;user-select:none}\n.zone.glow{box-shadow:0 0 14px 4px #0f0}\n.placed{display:inline-block;margin:3px;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:5px;background:#cde;font-size:13px}\n.note{color:#a05;font-size:13px}\n</style>\n\n<script>\nvar pred=null,phase=\"build\";\nvar parts=[\n{id:\"mem\",label:\"Cell Membrane\"},\n{id:\"cyto\",label:\"Cytoplasm\"},\n{id:\"ribo\",label:\"Ribosomes\"},\n{id:\"dna\",label:\"DNA/Genetic Material\"},\n{id:\"cap\",label:\"Protein Coat (Capsid)\"}\n];\nvar bact={},vir={},dragged=null;\n\nfunction lockPred(){\nvar r=document.querySelector('input[name=pred]:checked');\nif(!r){document.getElementById('predMsg').textContent=\" pick one!\";return;}\npred=r.value;document.getElementById('predMsg').textContent=\" Prediction recorded.\";\n}\n\nfunction renderTray(){\nvar t=document.getElementById('trayTokens');t.innerHTML=\"\";\nparts.forEach(function(p){\nvar d=document.createElement('span');d.className='tok';d.textContent=p.label;\nd.draggable=true;d.dataset.id=p.id;\nd.ondragstart=function(e){dragged=p.id;};\nt.appendChild(d);\n});\n}\nrenderTray();\n\ndocument.querySelectorAll('.zone').forEach(function(z){\nz.ondragover=function(e){e.preventDefault();};\nz.ondrop=function(e){e.preventDefault();drop(z.id);};\n});\n\nfunction drop(zoneId){\nif(!dragged)return;\nvar p=parts.find(x=>x.id===dragged);\nif(zoneId==='bactZone'){\nif(bact[dragged]){dragged=null;return;}\nbact[dragged]=true;addPlaced('bactZone',p.label);\nif(bact.mem&&bact.cyto&&bact.ribo&&bact.dna)document.getElementById('bactZone').classList.add('glow');\n}\nelse if(zoneId==='virZone'){\nif(['mem','cyto','ribo'].indexOf(dragged)>=0){\nflashNote('virZone','A virus has no cell parts');dragged=null;return;\n}\nif(vir[dragged]){dragged=null;return;}\nvir[dragged]=true;addPlaced('virZone',p.label);\n}\nelse if(zoneId==='hostZone'){\nif(dragged==='virionUnit'){\ndocument.getElementById('hostZone').dataset.hasvir=\"1\";\naddPlaced('hostZone','[VIRION inside]');\n}\n}\ndragged=null;\n}\n\nfunction addPlaced(zone,label){\nvar d=document.querySelector('#'+zone+' .drop');\nvar s=document.createElement('span');s.className='placed';s.textContent=label;d.appendChild(s);\n}\nfunction flashNote(zone,msg){\nvar d=document.querySelector('#'+zone+' .drop');\nvar s=document.createElement('span');s.className='note';s.textContent=\" ⤴ \"+msg;d.appendChild(s);\nsetTimeout(function(){s.remove();},1500);\n}\n\nfunction reproduce(){\nvar res=document.getElementById('result');\nif(phase===\"build\"){\nvar bOk=bact.mem&&bact.cyto&&bact.ribo&&bact.dna;\nvar vOk=vir.cap&&vir.dna;\nif(!bOk){res.style.color='#a05';res.textContent=\"Bacterium incomplete: needs membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes, DNA.\";return;}\nif(!vOk){res.style.color='#a05';res.textContent=\"Virion incomplete: needs Protein Coat + Genetic Material.\";return;}\nres.style.color='#080';\nres.innerHTML=\"Bacterium ✓ splits into two! &nbsp; | &nbsp; <span style='color:#a05'>Virion ✗ : No ribosomes → cannot make proteins → cannot copy itself.</span>\";\n// show host, make virion draggable\ndocument.getElementById('hostWrap').style.display='block';\nvar t=document.getElementById('trayTokens');t.innerHTML=\"\";\nvar d=document.createElement('span');d.className='tok';d.style.background='#f9c';\nd.textContent=\"🦠 VIRION (drag into HOST)\";d.draggable=true;\nd.ondragstart=function(){dragged='virionUnit';};\nt.appendChild(d);\ndocument.getElementById('repBtn').textContent=\"Reproduce in Host ▶\";\nphase=\"host\";\n}\nelse if(phase===\"host\"){\nif(document.getElementById('hostZone').dataset.hasvir!==\"1\"){\nres.style.color='#a05';res.textContent=\"Put the virion INSIDE the host cell first.\";return;}\nres.style.color='#080';\nres.innerHTML=\"Virion ✓ SUCCESS! Copies burst out of the host — <i>Used the host's ribosomes</i>. 🦠🦠🦠🦠\";\ndoReveal();\ndocument.getElementById('repBtn').disabled=true;\n}\n}\n\nfunction doReveal(){\nvar r=document.getElementById('reveal');\nif(pred==='Virion'||pred==='Both'){\nr.textContent=\"Surprised? A virion is NOT a tiny living cell — it can only copy itself by hijacking a host.\";\n}else if(pred==='Bacterium'){\nr.style.color='#080';\nr.textContent=\"You predicted the bacterium — correct! Only the bacterium reproduces alone.\";\n}else{\nr.textContent=\"A virion has no cell parts (no membrane, cytoplasm, or ribosomes) and needs a host to reproduce.\";\n}\n}\n</script>\n</div>",
                "status": "ready",
                "followUp": {
                  "enabled": true,
                  "type": "short",
                  "question": "A scientist puts a single flu virion alone in a tube of nutrients (sugars, salts, water) but no cells. Will the virion make copies of itself? Explain why or why not.",
                  "options": [
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                  ],
                  "answer": "No — it will not reproduce.",
                  "explanation": "A virion is only genetic material inside a protein coat. It has no ribosomes, cytoplasm, or membrane, so it cannot make proteins or copy itself. It can only reproduce by hijacking a living host cell to use that cell's machinery — nutrients alone are not enough."
                },
                "interactionKind": "process"
              },
              "quiz": {
                "items": []
              },
              "covers": [
                "n2",
                "n3"
              ],
              "role": "development",
              "sourceHint": "Characteristics of Viruses — 'they must depend on a host cell to synthesize their proteins and to make copies of themselves.'",
              "figureIds": [],
              "installsAha": [
                "aha1"
              ],
              "buildStatus": "done"
            },
            {
              "id": "sec_msgqjc7db5v7",
              "title": "Is a Virus Alive? You Decide",
              "type": "h5",
              "summary": "'Although viruses are not classified as living things, they share two important traits with living things... How would you classify viruses?'",
              "purpose": "Students sort shared and missing life-traits into columns and defend a verdict, discovering the borderline nature of viruses.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": []
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "Build a 'Is a Virus Alive? You Decide' interactive judgment board that forces the student to CONSTRUCT the insight that viruses are genuinely borderline (aha2). LAYOUT: top shows title and a shuffled tray of 6 draggable trait cards, each with an icon and short text: (a) 'Has genetic material (DNA or RNA)', (b) 'Can evolve over time', (c) 'Is NOT made of cells — no membrane, cytoplasm, or organelles', (d) 'Cannot reproduce on its own — needs a host cell', (e) 'Made only of a protein coat around nucleic acid', (f) 'Cannot make its own proteins'. Below the tray are TWO drop columns: LEFT 'Like living things' and RIGHT 'Not like living things'. STEP 1 PREDICT: before any dragging, ask the student to click a big gauge/slider prediction: 'Do you think there's an obvious yes/no answer?' (Definitely alive / Definitely not alive / It's a judgment call) and record it. STEP 2 SORT: student drags each of the 6 cards into a column. Correct placement: a,b -> 'Like living things'; c,d,e,f -> 'Not like living things'. On each drop, show a green check + one-line explanation drawn from the source (e.g. 'They have genetic material and can evolve — traits of living things'; 'Viruses are not cells and depend on a host to reproduce'). Wrong drops snap back with a gentle hint. STEP 3 REVEAL BALANCE: once all 6 are placed, animate a balance scale weighing the two columns — it visibly does NOT tip decisively (2 strong life-traits vs 4 non-life-traits, but the life-traits are the ones biologists debate), and display the framing line: 'Both columns have real evidence — that's why scientists still argue about this.' STEP 4 VERDICT: student must select a verdict button (Alive / Not alive / On the border) AND type at least one supporting trait from the correct column into a defense box before 'Submit verdict' unlocks. On submit, compare to their STEP-1 prediction and show a reflection: if they predicted 'obvious' but now cite trade-offs, surface 'You started thinking it was obvious — now you're citing evidence on both sides. That's the real answer: it's a judgment call.' Any verdict is accepted as long as it's defended with a valid trait. Use only content from this source (n5 shared traits; n6 controversy). No new vocabulary beyond the reading.",
                "html": "<div style=\"max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;color:#1a2733;padding:14px\">\n<style>\n#vb *{box-sizing:border-box}\n#vb h2{color:#0b5d78;margin:6px 0}\n.card{display:inline-block;background:#fff;border:2px solid #2a9d8f;border-radius:10px;padding:8px 10px;margin:5px;cursor:grab;font-size:13px;max-width:180px;vertical-align:top;box-shadow:0 2px 4px #0002}\n.card .ic{font-size:18px;margin-right:4px}\n.cols{display:flex;gap:12px;margin-top:10px}\n.col{flex:1;min-height:150px;border:2px dashed #7aa;border-radius:10px;padding:8px;background:#f2f9fb}\n.col h3{margin:4px 0;text-align:center;font-size:14px}\n.left{border-color:#2a9d8f}.right{border-color:#e76f51}\n.step{background:#eef6f9;border-left:4px solid #0b5d78;padding:8px 10px;margin:12px 0;border-radius:6px}\n.expl{font-size:12px;color:#0a6b3f;margin:2px 6px;display:block}\nbutton{background:#0b5d78;color:#fff;border:0;padding:8px 14px;border-radius:8px;cursor:pointer;font-size:14px;margin:3px}\nbutton:disabled{background:#9bb;cursor:not-allowed}\n.pred{background:#fff;border:2px solid #0b5d78;color:#0b5d78}\n.pred.sel{background:#0b5d78;color:#fff}\n.verd{background:#fff;border:2px solid #e76f51;color:#e76f51}\n.verd.sel{background:#e76f51;color:#fff}\ntextarea{width:100%;border:2px solid #ccc;border-radius:8px;padding:6px;font-size:13px}\n#scale{height:110px;position:relative;margin:10px 0}\n.hint{color:#e76f51;font-size:12px}\n</style>\n<div id=\"vb\">\n<h2>Is a Virus Alive? You Decide</h2>\n\n<div class=\"step\"><b>Step 1 — Predict:</b> Do you think there's an obvious yes/no answer?<br>\n<button class=\"pred\" data-p=\"Definitely alive\">Definitely alive</button>\n<button class=\"pred\" data-p=\"Definitely not alive\">Definitely not alive</button>\n<button class=\"pred\" data-p=\"It's a judgment call\">It's a judgment call</button>\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"phase2\" style=\"display:none\">\n<div class=\"step\"><b>Step 2 — Sort</b> each card into a column. Wrong drops snap back with a hint.</div>\n<div id=\"tray\"></div>\n<div class=\"cols\">\n<div class=\"col left\" data-side=\"L\" ondragover=\"event.preventDefault()\"><h3>Like living things</h3></div>\n<div class=\"col right\" data-side=\"R\" ondragover=\"event.preventDefault()\"><h3>Not like living things</h3></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"hint\" id=\"hint\"></p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"step\" id=\"phase3\" style=\"display:none\">\n<b>Step 3 — Balance:</b>\n<div id=\"scale\"></div>\n<b id=\"balmsg\"></b>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"step\" id=\"phase4\" style=\"display:none\">\n<b>Step 4 — Verdict:</b> Pick a verdict and defend it with a trait from the correct column.<br>\n<button class=\"verd\" data-v=\"Alive\">Alive</button>\n<button class=\"verd\" data-v=\"Not alive\">Not alive</button>\n<button class=\"verd\" data-v=\"On the border\">On the border</button>\n<p style=\"font-size:12px;margin:4px 0\">Type at least one supporting trait:</p>\n<textarea id=\"defense\" rows=\"2\" placeholder=\"e.g. Viruses have genetic material and can evolve...\"></textarea>\n<button id=\"submit\" disabled>Submit verdict</button>\n<div id=\"reflect\" style=\"margin-top:8px\"></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<script>\nvar data=[\n{id:'a',ic:'🧬',t:'Has genetic material (DNA or RNA)',side:'L',e:'They have genetic material — a trait of living things.'},\n{id:'b',ic:'🔄',t:'Can evolve over time',side:'L',e:'They can evolve — a trait of living things.'},\n{id:'c',ic:'🚫',t:'Is NOT made of cells — no membrane, cytoplasm, or organelles',side:'R',e:'Viruses are not made of cells.'},\n{id:'d',ic:'🏠',t:'Cannot reproduce on its own — needs a host cell',side:'R',e:'They depend on a host to reproduce.'},\n{id:'e',ic:'🥚',t:'Made only of a protein coat around nucleic acid',side:'R',e:'Just a protein coat around nucleic acid — not cellular.'},\n{id:'f',ic:'⛔',t:'Cannot make its own proteins',side:'R',e:'They cannot make their own proteins.'}\n];\nvar prediction=null,verdict=null,placed=0;\nvar vb=document.getElementById('vb');\ndocument.querySelectorAll('.pred').forEach(function(b){b.onclick=function(){\ndocument.querySelectorAll('.pred').forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('sel'));\nb.classList.add('sel');prediction=b.dataset.p;\ndocument.getElementById('phase2').style.display='block';\n};});\n\n// shuffle\nvar order=data.slice().sort(()=>Math.random()-0.5);\nvar tray=document.getElementById('tray');\norder.forEach(function(d){\nvar c=document.createElement('div');c.className='card';c.draggable=true;c.id='c_'+d.id;\nc.innerHTML='<span class=\"ic\">'+d.ic+'</span>'+d.t;\nc.ondragstart=function(e){e.dataTransfer.setData('t',d.id);};\ntray.appendChild(c);\n});\ndocument.querySelectorAll('.col').forEach(function(col){\ncol.ondrop=function(e){\ne.preventDefault();\nvar id=e.dataTransfer.getData('t');\nvar d=data.find(x=>x.id==id);var card=document.getElementById('c_'+id);\nif(!d||!card)return;\nif(d.side==col.dataset.side){\ncard.draggable=false;card.style.cursor='default';card.style.borderColor='#2a9d8f';\nvar ex=document.createElement('span');ex.className='expl';ex.textContent='✅ '+d.e;\ncard.appendChild(ex);\ncol.appendChild(card);\ndocument.getElementById('hint').textContent='';\nplaced++;\nif(placed==6)showBalance();\n}else{\ndocument.getElementById('hint').textContent='Not quite — think about whether that trait matches this column. Card returned to tray.';\n}\n};\n});\n\nfunction showBalance(){\nvar p3=document.getElementById('phase3');p3.style.display='block';\nvar s=document.getElementById('scale');\ns.innerHTML='<div style=\"position:absolute;left:50%;top:0;width:4px;height:100px;background:#555;transform:translateX(-50%)\"></div>'+\n'<div id=\"beam\" style=\"position:absolute;left:50%;top:8px;width:70%;height:6px;background:#333;transform:translate(-50%,0) rotate(0deg);transition:transform 1.2s\"></div>'+\n'<div id=\"pl\" style=\"position:absolute;left:16%;top:40px;background:#2a9d8f;color:#fff;padding:6px 8px;border-radius:8px;font-size:12px;transition:top 1.2s\">Life traits: 2</div>'+\n'<div id=\"pr\" style=\"position:absolute;right:16%;top:40px;background:#e76f51;color:#fff;padding:6px 8px;border-radius:8px;font-size:12px;transition:top 1.2s\">Non-life: 4</div>';\nsetTimeout(function(){\ndocument.getElementById('beam').style.transform='translate(-50%,0) rotate(6deg)';\ndocument.getElementById('pl').style.top='30px';\ndocument.getElementById('pr').style.top='55px';\n},400);\ndocument.getElementById('balmsg').textContent='It only barely tips — both columns have real evidence. That\\u2019s why scientists still argue about this.';\np3.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});\ndocument.getElementById('phase4').style.display='block';\n}\n\ndocument.querySelectorAll('.verd').forEach(function(b){b.onclick=function(){\ndocument.querySelectorAll('.verd').forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('sel'));\nb.classList.add('sel');verdict=b.dataset.v;check();\n};});\ndocument.getElementById('defense').oninput=check;\nfunction check(){\nvar txt=document.getElementById('defense').value.toLowerCase();\nvar valid=['genetic','evolve','cell','host','protein coat','nucleic','proteins','membrane','reproduce']\n.some(k=>txt.indexOf(k)>-1);\ndocument.getElementById('submit').disabled=!(verdict&&valid&&txt.length>8);\n}\ndocument.getElementById('submit').onclick=function(){\nvar txt=document.getElementById('defense').value.toLowerCase();\nvar tradeoff=/(both|border|debate|argue|but|however|host|coat|not.*cell)/.test(txt);\nvar msg='<b>Verdict recorded:</b> \"'+verdict+'\" — defended with real evidence. ✅<br>';\nif(prediction!==\"It's a judgment call\"){\nmsg+='<p style=\"color:#0b5d78\"><b>Reflection:</b> You started thinking it was obvious (\"'+prediction+'\") — now you\\u2019re citing evidence on both sides. That\\u2019s the real answer: <b>it\\u2019s a judgment call.</b> Viruses have genetic material and can evolve (life traits), yet aren\\u2019t cells and can\\u2019t reproduce alone — genuinely borderline.</p>';\n}else{\nmsg+='<p style=\"color:#0b5d78\"><b>Reflection:</b> Your prediction held up — and now you have the evidence to back it. Viruses sit on the border: they share genetic material and evolution with living things but lack cells and independent reproduction.</p>';\n}\ndocument.getElementById('reflect').innerHTML=msg;\nthis.disabled=true;\ndocument.getElementById('reflect').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});\n};\n</script>\n</div>",
                "status": "ready",
                "followUp": {
                  "enabled": true,
                  "type": "short",
                  "question": "A newly discovered microbe has its own genetic material and can evolve, but it is not made of cells and cannot copy itself without invading another organism's cells. Based on how we reasoned about viruses, is it clearly 'alive'? Explain using one trait from each side.",
                  "options": [
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                  ],
                  "answer": "No — it's a borderline/judgment call. It shares life-traits (genetic material, can evolve) but lacks others (not a cell, can't reproduce on its own), so there's no obvious yes/no answer.",
                  "explanation": "This re-skins the virus question with a new organism to check that the student learned the transferable move: 'alive vs not' isn't automatic. Because the microbe has genes and evolves (living-like) yet isn't a cell and can't self-reproduce (not living-like), the evidence is split — exactly why classifying viruses is controversial (n6), driven by the two shared traits (n5)."
                },
                "interactionKind": "explore"
              },
              "quiz": {
                "items": []
              },
              "covers": [
                "n5",
                "n6"
              ],
              "role": "development",
              "sourceHint": "'Although viruses are not classified as living things, they share two important traits with living things... How would you classify viruses?'",
              "figureIds": [],
              "installsAha": [
                "aha2"
              ],
              "buildStatus": "done"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ch_msgqjc7d5bpn",
          "title": "Structure & Classification of Viruses",
          "summary": "Break down virus structure (capsid, nucleic acid, envelope) and learn to classify any virus by three independent traits.",
          "sections": [
            {
              "id": "sec_msgqjc7dgda3",
              "title": "Inside a Virus: Capsid, Nucleic Acid, Envelope",
              "type": "reading",
              "summary": "Structure of Viruses passage — capsid, envelope of phospholipids/proteins, receptor molecules.",
              "purpose": "Explain the capsid protein coat, the DNA-or-RNA core, and the optional envelope with its immune-evading receptors.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": [
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqkbvheb8r",
                    "title": "The Capsid: A Protein Coat",
                    "html": "<p>Every virus has a <strong>capsid</strong> — a protective coat made of protein. It wraps around and shields the virus's genetic material inside. The capsid's shape can differ from one virus to another.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "In your own words, what job does the capsid do?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "It is the protein coat that protects the virus's genetic material inside.",
                      "explanation": "The capsid is a protective protein shell around the nucleic acid."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "Simple cross-section of a virus showing an outer protein capsid surrounding an inner coiled strand of nucleic acid, labeled 'capsid' and 'nucleic acid'"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqkbvhdkhk",
                    "title": "DNA or RNA — Never Both",
                    "html": "<p>Inside the capsid sits the virus's genetic material. A virus carries <strong>either DNA or RNA</strong> — but never both at the same time. This single molecule holds the instructions the virus needs.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "A newly discovered virus contains RNA. Could it also contain DNA?",
                      "options": [
                        "Yes, all viruses have both",
                        "No, a virus carries either DNA or RNA, never both",
                        "Only if it has an envelope",
                        "Only if it is large"
                      ],
                      "answer": "No, a virus carries either DNA or RNA, never both",
                      "explanation": "Each virus contains just one type of nucleic acid."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqkbvh9no9",
                    "title": "The Optional Envelope",
                    "html": "<p>Some viruses — but not all — have an extra outer layer called an <strong>envelope</strong>, made of phospholipids and proteins. Surprisingly, the virus steals this envelope from portions of its <em>host's own cell membrane</em>. It surrounds the capsid.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "Where does a virus's envelope come from?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "From portions of the host cell's membrane.",
                      "explanation": "The envelope is built from the host cell membrane, not made by the virus alone."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "Diagram of an enveloped virus: capsid in the center, surrounded by a phospholipid bilayer envelope studded with protein spikes"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqkbvhjrny",
                    "title": "Why the Envelope Matters",
                    "html": "<p>The envelope does two useful jobs for the virus. Because it is made from the host's own membrane, it helps <strong>hide the virus from the host's immune system</strong>. It can also carry <strong>receptor molecules</strong> that bind to host cells — making it easier for the virus to break in and infect them.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "Why can an enveloped virus be harder for the immune system to catch?",
                      "options": [
                        "Its envelope is made from the host's own membrane, so it looks familiar",
                        "It has no genetic material",
                        "It is larger than a cell",
                        "It reproduces on its own"
                      ],
                      "answer": "Its envelope is made from the host's own membrane, so it looks familiar",
                      "explanation": "Because the envelope comes from the host's membrane, the immune system may not recognize the virus as foreign."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgqkbvhtrxv",
                    "title": "Three Independent Switches",
                    "html": "<p>Here's the big idea: you don't have to memorize virus families as whole names. Instead, you <strong>read three independent traits</strong>: (1) capsid shape, (2) envelope — yes or no, and (3) DNA or RNA. Each trait is like a separate switch you can set on its own.</p><p>Combined together, these three switches point to the virus family. Change one switch and you may land on a different family.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "Virus X: helical capsid, has an envelope, carries RNA. Which three traits did you just read to identify it, and why don't you need to memorize its family name first?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "I read capsid shape (helical), envelope (yes), and nucleic acid (RNA). These three independent traits together point to the family, so I can decode any virus by checking the switches instead of memorizing names.",
                      "explanation": "This matches aha3: classification comes from three independent traits combined, not from memorizing each family as a whole."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": "A virus 'builder' panel with three toggle dials: capsid shape (helical/icosahedral/complex), envelope on-off, and nucleic acid (DNA/RNA), with the assembled virus updating to reveal a family name"
                  }
                ]
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "",
                "html": "",
                "status": "idle",
                "followUp": null
              },
              "quiz": {
                "items": []
              },
              "covers": [
                "n7",
                "n9",
                "n10",
                "n11"
              ],
              "role": "development",
              "sourceHint": "Structure of Viruses passage — capsid, envelope of phospholipids/proteins, receptor molecules.",
              "figureIds": [],
              "installsAha": [
                "aha3"
              ],
              "buildStatus": "done"
            },
            {
              "id": "sec_msgqjc7dbqsy",
              "title": "Capsid Shapes in 3D",
              "type": "vr",
              "summary": "picture_01 — three labeled capsid structures (helical, icosahedral, complex).",
              "purpose": "Students explore helical, icosahedral, and complex capsids as 3D models to internalize structural variation.",
              "vr": {
                "scene": {
