      "ahaKeys": [
        {
          "id": "aha1",
          "insight": "A virus is not a cell — it's just genetic material inside a protein coat, so it CAN'T reproduce by itself and must hijack a host cell.",
          "whyKey": "Every question about why viruses cause disease, need a host, or aren't 'alive' collapses to this one structural fact; owning it lets a student reason about any virus, not memorize each one.",
          "misconception": "Students think a virus is a very tiny living cell or germ that grows and multiplies on its own.",
          "buildIdea": "Give the student a 'cell parts checklist' (membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes, DNA) and two draggable objects — a bacterium and a virion. They drop parts into each; the virion only accepts genetic material + coat, then when they hit 'reproduce' the lone virion fails until dragged inside a host cell, where it succeeds. Insight emerges from the failed vs successful reproduction.",
          "nodeIds": [
            "n1",
            "n2",
            "n3",
            "n4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "aha2",
          "insight": "Viruses sit on the border of life: they have genetic material and can evolve like living things, but they aren't cells and can't self-reproduce — so whether they're 'alive' is a real judgment call.",
          "whyKey": "The 'is a virus alive?' short-answer question is unanswerable by memorization; students must weigh shared-vs-missing traits, a transferable reasoning move.",
          "misconception": "Students think 'alive vs not alive' is a simple yes/no with an obvious answer for viruses.",
          "buildIdea": "A two-column sorting board 'Like living things' / 'Not like living things'; the student drags traits (has genes, evolves, no cells, can't reproduce alone) into columns, then must pick a verdict and defend it — the balanced columns force them to see it's genuinely borderline.",
          "nodeIds": [
            "n5",
            "n6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "aha3",
          "insight": "You classify a virus by reading three independent 'switches' — capsid shape, envelope yes/no, and DNA vs RNA — combined together they name the family.",
          "whyKey": "Any re-skinned classification question (new virus, new table) is solved by checking these three traits; students stop memorizing families and start reading traits.",
          "misconception": "Students think each virus family must be memorized as a whole name rather than decoded from a few independent traits.",
          "buildIdea": "A 3D virus builder with three toggles: capsid shape (helical / icosahedral / complex), envelope on-off, and nucleic acid (DNA/RNA). As the student sets the switches to match a disease card (e.g. 'Flu'), the model rebuilds and reveals the family — showing traits are independent dials that together determine identity.",
          "nodeIds": [
            "n7",
            "n8",
            "n9",
            "n10",
            "n11",
            "n12",
            "n13"
          ]
        }
      ],
